### ## Local History - Your Hidden Safety Net

Beyond the standard Undo and Redo, VS Code also provides **Local History**. This automatically saves versions of your files as you work.

If you make a change, save, and then later realise you need to go back to an earlier version, you can view the Local History timeline and restore any previous saved state - even after closing and reopening the file.

This is particularly valuable for documentation authors. You might make a series of changes over a week, save regularly, open and close the file or vscode multiple times and then decide you want to revert to how things were 5 days ago. With Local History, you can do that easily, with Undo you can't. Once you close a file or vscode all Undos are lost.

**In short:** Local History silently preserves every saved version of your files, giving you a recoverable timeline that survives between sessions.

